 or $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-129836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-129836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-129836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ or $1$"] ["text" "$-1$ or $2$"] ["text" "$-2$ or $1$"] ["text" "$-2$ or $2$"] ["text" "$1$ or $2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-129836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-1$ or $2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-14y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-7$ or $\\lambda =2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-1$ or $14$"] ["text" "$-14$ or $1$"] ["text" "$-2$ or $7$"] ["text" "$-4$ or $9$"] ["text" "$-7$ or $2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-7$ or $2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90720" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{6x}+B{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+15y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =5.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{7x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-7783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+24y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4-{e}^{6x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}+2{e}^{4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x+1}"] ["math" "{e}^{6x-4}"] ["math" "{e}^{6x}-8{e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90729" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4-{e}^{6x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{x}"] ["math" "3{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "6{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{e}^{2(x+4)}"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90727" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{3x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "3{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "4{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "5{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "9{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-90725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9{e}^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7358f48c44a6e848b459e480d470fb76ec9957b59abeb9cb4b826a20a1718df3"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "5{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "5{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "q-130124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/state.json" "file_sha256" "caef25b9f18f90f83779c133e43ee6325e9f751b5be7c3b364830cdabd8f116e"} {"question" "e-6587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6587.json" "file_sha256" "7cea617564502926714ab0a107058239b34e31ed43df8919a37fa3dafc37ff3c"} {"question" "e-6587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 -  3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and $\\lambda =  -1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6587.json" "file_sha256" "7cea617564502926714ab0a107058239b34e31ed43df8919a37fa3dafc37ff3c"} {"question" "e-6588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6588.json" "file_sha256" "7415d71b202fb1a4340b0ff266eb689f88b4fe36f4c8a1486f1e633aa4fd335c"} {"question" "e-6588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1$ and $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6588.json" "file_sha256" "7415d71b202fb1a4340b0ff266eb689f88b4fe36f4c8a1486f1e633aa4fd335c"} {"question" "e-6589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\n- $-3e^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\n- $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6589.json" "file_sha256" "45fe3343160011fba3f8f957c98b7d245b38f315f50f9b982c794926047c35e9"} {"question" "e-6589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063643/example-6589.json" "file_sha256" "45fe3343160011fba3f8f957c98b7d245b38f315f50f9b982c794926047c35e9"}] "usage" {"e-6588" [] "e-6587" [] "e-6589" []} "basis" 2055 "retractions" [[17592186078258 :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t)$. If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda$?"] [17592186078258 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 3λe^{λt} - 4e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 3λ - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$"] [17592186078259 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0$."] [17592186078259 :question/wor